      content:
          'A 35-year-old male with C5 ASIA C incomplete spinal cord injury sustained 3 years ago in an all-terrain vehicle accident presents to the spasticity clinic with severe lower extremity spasticity that is interfering with his function, positioning, and quality of life. He has bilateral lower extremity spasticity that is most severe in the hip adductors and knee extensors. He is unable to perform self-catheterization due to adductor spasm, his wife cannot provide perineal care, and he is developing a right hip adductor contracture. He has tried oral baclofen up to 80 mg daily, tizanidine 24 mg daily, and diazepam 5 mg TID without adequate relief. The high doses of oral medications cause excessive drowsiness that impairs his participation in therapy and his ability to work as a call center representative from home.',
