    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **SCI Level/Classification**: C5 ASIA C (incomplete) x 3 years; motor incomplete with some voluntary movement in bilateral lower extremities (hip flexion 2/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5)\n- **Spasticity Distribution**: Bilateral lower extremities >> upper extremities; worst in hip adductors, knee extensors, and ankle plantarflexors\n- **Current Anti-Spasticity Medications**: Baclofen 80 mg/day (20 mg QID), tizanidine 8 mg TID, diazepam 5 mg TID — all at maximum tolerated doses with inadequate spasticity control and dose-limiting sedation\n- **Functional Impact of Spasticity**: Cannot perform intermittent catheterization independently (adductor spasm), wife unable to provide perineal hygiene, painful spasms wake him 3-4 times/night, wheelchair positioning difficult (extensor thrusting pushes him out of chair), developing right hip adductor contracture (passive abduction limited to 15 degrees)\n- **Beneficial Aspects of Spasticity**: Uses extensor tone in bilateral lower extremities to assist with standing pivot transfers (a concern if spasticity is eliminated entirely)\n- **Botulinum Toxin History**: Has received 3 rounds of botulinum toxin A (onabotulinumtoxinA) injections to bilateral hip adductors and hamstrings — provided 6-8 weeks of partial relief each time, but insufficient and requiring increasingly high doses approaching the maximum recommended dose\n- **Functional Status**: Uses power wheelchair; requires moderate assist for transfers (standing pivot using extensor tone); modified independent in upper body ADLs; dependent for lower body ADLs/catheterization due to spasticity\n- **Employment**: Works from home as a call center representative (requires alertness — sedation from oral meds impairing performance)\n- **PMH**: Neurogenic bladder (on IC program when possible, otherwise indwelling catheter), neurogenic bowel (reflexic program), history of autonomic dysreflexia',
