    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the ITB trial protocol and patient selection criteria. How do you differentiate spasticity from spasm, and why does it matter for treatment?',
      content:
          '**Patient Selection Criteria for ITB Pump**:\n1. **Severe spasticity** of spinal or cerebral origin (Modified Ashworth Scale ≥3 in affected muscle groups) — met\n2. **Failed adequate trials** of at least 2-3 oral anti-spasticity medications at maximum tolerated doses — met (baclofen, tizanidine, diazepam)\n3. **Functional impairment** from spasticity (hygiene, positioning, mobility, pain, sleep) — clearly met\n4. **Medically stable** for surgical implantation — yes\n5. **No active infection**, especially skin infection near potential pump pocket site\n6. **Adequate body habitus** for pump implantation (may be challenging in very thin patients)\n7. **Realistic expectations** — patient and caregivers understand the device, maintenance requirements, and limitations\n8. **Access to a pump management center** for refills, dose adjustments, and emergencies\n\n**ITB Trial Protocol**:\n1. **Purpose**: Demonstrate that intrathecal baclofen is effective before committing to surgical implantation\n2. **Method**: **Lumbar puncture** (typically L3-4 or L4-5) with injection of a **bolus dose of baclofen** into the intrathecal space\n3. **Starting dose**: Typically **50 mcg** for spinal spasticity (25 mcg for cerebral spasticity)\n4. **Monitoring**: Modified Ashworth Scale assessed at baseline and every 1-2 hours for 4-8 hours post-injection. Also monitor Penn Spasm Frequency Scale, vital signs, and subjective symptom relief.\n5. **Positive trial**: ≥1 point decrease in MAS score in targeted muscle groups within 4-8 hours of injection. Peak effect at 4 hours for bolus injection.\n6. **If 50 mcg is ineffective**: Repeat trial on a separate day with **75 mcg**, then **100 mcg** if needed. If 100 mcg produces no response, the patient is likely not a candidate.\n7. **Monitoring for complications**: Respiratory depression, excessive hypotonia, hypotension, headache (post-lumbar puncture), nausea.\n\n**Spasticity vs. Spasm — Important Distinction**:\n- **Spasticity**: Velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone due to upper motor neuron lesion. Measured by MAS. Continuous phenomenon worsened by rapid passive stretch.\n- **Spasm**: Involuntary phasic muscle contractions (flexor or extensor). Measured by Penn Spasm Frequency Scale. Episodic, often triggered by stimuli (touch, position change, bladder distension).\n- **Why it matters**: ITB is highly effective for BOTH spasticity and spasms, but some treatments (e.g., botulinum toxin) primarily address spasticity/tone without significantly reducing spasm frequency. Oral baclofen and diazepam address both. ITB allows more targeted dosing to treat both components with less systemic sedation.\n- **Document both separately**: Before and after treatment, track MAS (spasticity) AND Penn Spasm Frequency Scale (spasms) independently.',
