    Section(
      title: '+ The ITB trial shows a dramatic response — MAS decreases from 3 to 1 in all tested muscle groups within 3 hours. The patient proceeds with pump implantation. Describe the pump system and the dose titration process.',
      content:
          '**ITB Pump System Components**:\n1. **Pump**: Programmable, battery-powered pump implanted subcutaneously in the **anterior abdominal wall** (usually left lower quadrant). Contains a drug reservoir (typically 20-40 mL capacity). Battery life approximately **7 years** (newer models may be longer). Common models: Medtronic SynchroMed II.\n2. **Catheter**: Silicone catheter tunneled subcutaneously from the pump to the **lumbar intrathecal space** (typically entering at L3-4). Tip positioned at the **thoracolumbar junction (T10-L1)** for lower extremity spasticity targeting.\n3. **Programmer**: External device used by the clinician to adjust dose, delivery mode, and refill schedule wirelessly through the skin.\n\n**Dose Titration Process**:\n1. **Starting dose**: Typically **twice the effective trial dose** divided over 24 hours as a continuous infusion. If 50 mcg bolus was effective, start at approximately **100 mcg/day continuous infusion**.\n2. **Titration schedule**: Increase by **10-15% every 24-48 hours** during inpatient stay (first 3-5 days). Outpatient adjustments at clinic visits every 2-4 weeks thereafter.\n3. **Dose range**: Most spinal spasticity patients stabilize at **200-800 mcg/day**. Cerebral spasticity may require lower doses.\n4. **Delivery modes**:\n- **Simple continuous**: Same rate 24 hours/day\n- **Flex dosing**: Different rates programmed for different times of day (e.g., lower dose during the day to preserve some tone for transfers, higher dose at night for sleep and positioning)\n- **Bolus dosing**: Patient-activated boluses for breakthrough spasms (if enabled)\n\n**For this patient**: Consider **flex dosing** — lower daytime dose to preserve some extensor tone for standing pivot transfers, higher nighttime dose to reduce spasms that wake him.\n\n**Refill Schedule**:\n- Pump reservoir requires **percutaneous refill** through the skin into the pump\'s drug port\n- Refill interval depends on dose and reservoir size — typically every **1-6 months**\n- Refill performed in clinic using sterile technique with a specialized refill kit\n- **Never let the pump run empty** — abrupt baclofen withdrawal is a medical emergency\n\n**Critical Safety Information — ITB Withdrawal**:\n- **Life-threatening emergency**: Abrupt discontinuation of intrathecal baclofen (pump malfunction, catheter kink/break, missed refill) causes: rebound severe spasticity, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, multi-organ failure, and death\n- Resembles neuroleptic malignant syndrome/malignant hyperthermia\n- **Emergency management**: High-dose oral baclofen (80-120 mg/day divided), IV benzodiazepines, cyproheptadine, dantrolene; ICU admission; emergency pump interrogation and refill\n- **Prevention**: Never miss a refill appointment; pump has low-reservoir alarm',
