    Section(
      title: '+ How do you balance spasticity reduction with preservation of functional tone? Design the ongoing pump management and rehabilitation plan.',
      content:
          '**Balancing Spasticity Reduction with Function**:\n\nThis is one of the most critical considerations in ITB therapy. Complete elimination of spasticity is NOT always the goal — some tone may be functionally beneficial.\n\n**Functional Benefits of Spasticity to Preserve**:\n- Extensor tone assists standing pivot transfers (this patient)\n- Leg stiffness may assist with standing frame use\n- Some tone helps maintain muscle bulk and venous return\n- Protects against osteoporosis to some degree\n\n**Strategy — Flex Dosing Program**:\n- **Daytime (8 AM - 10 PM)**: Lower dose — target MAS 1-1+ in hip adductors (enough reduction for catheterization and hygiene) while preserving some extensor tone for transfers. Example: 150 mcg/day rate.\n- **Nighttime (10 PM - 8 AM)**: Higher dose — target MAS 0-1 for comfort, reduced spasms, improved sleep, and positioning. Example: 250 mcg/day rate.\n- **Transition periods**: Program gradual ramp-up/ramp-down over 30-60 minutes to avoid abrupt tone changes.\n\n**Transfer Training Adaptation**:\n- Physical therapy must retrain transfers with the new baseline tone level\n- May transition from standing pivot to **sliding board transfer** if standing is no longer feasible\n- Strengthen hip extensors and knee extensors to compensate for reduced spastic support\n- Consider a **standing frame program** for weight bearing, bone health, and functional standing if transfers change\n\n**Ongoing Pump Management Plan**:\n1. **Clinic visits**: Every 4-8 weeks initially for dose optimization; every 3-6 months once stable\n2. **Refill schedule**: Based on dose and reservoir volume; set recurring appointments and ensure patient never misses\n3. **Annual assessment**: Document MAS, Penn Spasm Scale, functional status (SCIM-III), skin integrity, and patient-reported outcomes at each visit\n4. **Catheter monitoring**: Fluoroscopy or CT to assess catheter position if there is sudden change in spasticity (may indicate catheter migration, kink, or disconnect)\n5. **Battery replacement**: Surgical replacement of pump when battery approaches end-of-life (approximately 7 years); plan elective replacement surgery in advance\n\n**Rehabilitation Program**:\n- **ROM program**: Continue daily stretching to maintain gains, especially hip abduction and ankle dorsiflexion. With reduced spasticity, ROM should be easier to maintain and improve.\n- **Strengthening**: Capitalize on improved motor control — with spasticity reduced, volitional movement may be more accessible. Progressive resistance training for hip flexors and knee extensors.\n- **Bladder management**: Now that adductor spasm is controlled, retrain intermittent catheterization independence\n- **Seating**: Reassess wheelchair seating — may no longer need aggressive positioning hardware (chest harness, pelvic strap) with reduced extensor thrusting',
    ),
