    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss pump emergency protocols, MRI safety, and practical considerations for living with an ITB pump.',
      content:
          '**Emergency Protocols — ITB Withdrawal is Life-Threatening**:\n\n1. **Patient/Caregiver Education Card**: Provide a wallet-sized card with:\n- Diagnosis and pump type/model\n- Current dose and refill schedule\n- Emergency contact numbers (pump management center, neurosurgeon)\n- Signs of withdrawal: rapidly increasing spasticity, fever, sweating, tachycardia, mental status changes\n- Signs of overdose: excessive drowsiness, respiratory depression, hypotension, flaccidity\n- Instructions for emergency personnel\n\n2. **Emergency Oral Baclofen Supply**: Prescribe oral baclofen to keep at home and carry when traveling. If pump malfunction is suspected, patient should take oral baclofen immediately (20-40 mg doses, escalate rapidly) and go to the emergency department.\n\n3. **Hospital Alert**: Ensure the patient\'s local emergency department has documentation of the ITB pump in their system. Provide a letter for the ER stating the pump type, current dose, and emergency management protocol.\n\n**MRI Safety**:\n- The **Medtronic SynchroMed II pump is MRI Conditional** under specific conditions:\n  - 1.5 Tesla MRI only (3T is generally NOT approved)\n  - Pump must be interrogated before AND after MRI\n  - Pump should be stopped (suspended) before entering the MRI scanner\n  - Specific absorption rate (SAR) limits must be followed\n  - Pump must be restarted and dose verified after MRI\n- **The patient should ALWAYS inform radiology staff about the pump before any MRI**\n- CT scans and X-rays are safe and unaffected by the pump\n\n**Travel Considerations**:\n- **Air travel**: The pump is safe for commercial air travel. Airport metal detectors and security scanners will detect the pump — carry the medical device card and physician letter.\n- **Altitude changes**: Theoretical concern for dose changes with significant altitude variation (drug delivery changes with pressure) — generally not clinically significant for commercial flight altitudes.\n- **Refill planning**: Schedule refills before travel. Identify a pump management center near the travel destination for trips lasting longer than the refill interval.\n- **International travel**: Carry a physician letter in English and the local language, current medication list, and emergency contact information.\n\n**Day-to-Day Considerations**:\n- **Pump site care**: Keep the surgical site clean and dry until fully healed. Monitor for signs of infection (redness, warmth, drainage, fever).\n- **Physical activity**: Avoid contact sports or activities with risk of abdominal trauma. Swimming, wheelchair sports (with abdominal protection), and exercise are generally safe.\n- **Pump alarm**: The pump has an audible alarm that sounds when reservoir is low or battery is depleted. Patient must respond to alarms promptly.\n- **Seatbelt**: Shoulder/lap belt may need padding over the pump site for comfort.\n- **Skin**: Pump pocket area may be visible as a mild abdominal bulge. Clothing adjustment may be needed.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'Before the ITB trial, the patient expresses significant anxiety. He says: \'I don\'t know about having a machine implanted inside me. What if it breaks? What if it makes things worse? I\'ve already been through so much with this injury. I\'m not sure I can handle another surgery.\'',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How would you address this patient\'s fears about ITB pump implantation?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the fear and acknowledge what he has been through**:\n\'Your concerns are completely reasonable, and I want you to know that hesitancy is normal. You have been through a major life-changing injury and years of managing your spasticity with medications that are not working well enough. Adding another medical procedure to that is a lot to consider. I do not take that lightly.\'\n\n**2. Clarify the trial as a "test drive"**:\n\'Here is something important that I want to emphasize: we are not asking you to commit to surgery today. The first step is a trial — a single injection in your back similar to a lumbar puncture. It wears off in a few hours. If it works and you feel significantly better, then we talk about the pump. If it does not work or you do not like how it feels, we do not proceed. You are completely in control of this decision at every step.\'\n\n**3. Address the "machine inside me" concern directly**:\n\'I understand the idea of having a device implanted feels foreign and maybe even frightening. The pump is about the size of a hockey puck and sits just under the skin on your abdomen. Most patients tell me that after the first few weeks, they forget it is there. It is not visible to others under your clothing, and it does not make noise or produce any sensation during normal operation. Many patients describe it as "the best decision I ever made" because of how much their quality of life improved.\'\n\n**4. Address the fear of malfunction honestly**:\n\'You asked what happens if it breaks. That is a fair question, and I want to be honest. Like any medical device, there are risks — the catheter can occasionally move or become blocked, and the pump will eventually need battery replacement after about 7 years. These are manageable situations that we monitor for at your regular clinic visits. If the pump ever stops working, there is an emergency protocol involving oral medication to keep you safe while we fix the problem. We will train you and your wife on exactly what to do.\'\n\n**5. Reframe in terms of his current situation**:\n\'Right now, the medications you are on are causing drowsiness that is affecting your work and your daily life. The spasticity is preventing you from doing your own catheterization, it is disrupting your sleep, and it is causing a contracture in your hip. The pump delivers medication directly to the spinal fluid at a tiny fraction of the oral dose — so you get better spasticity control with dramatically less sedation. For many patients, it is the difference between being alert and engaged versus spending the day in a fog.\'\n\n**6. Offer connection with another pump patient**:\n\'I know that hearing from me as your doctor is one thing, but hearing from someone who has actually lived with a pump is another. I have a patient who had a very similar situation to yours — he was nervous before the trial but agreed to go ahead, and he would be happy to talk with you about his experience. Would you be open to that?\'\n\n**7. Give time and autonomy**:\n\'There is absolutely no rush on this decision. Take the time you need to think about it, talk it over with your wife, write down your questions, and come back when you are ready. I will be here whenever you are.\'\n\n**Key principles**: Validating fear, clarifying the trial as low-commitment, honest discussion of risks, reframing current suffering versus intervention risk, peer connection, patient autonomy and no time pressure.',
